-- title:
--   A closed cube lies in the ball of radius half the diagonal about its centre
-- statement:
--   Let $d \in \mathbb{N}$, let $h \in \mathbb{R}$ and let $u \in \mathbb{R}^d$. With $\bar A_h(u)$ the closed cube of side $h$ centred at $u$, and with $\mathbb{R}^d$ carrying the Euclidean norm,
--
--   $$\bar A_h(u) \;\subseteq\; \bar B\Bigl(u,\; \frac{h}{2}\sqrt{d}\Bigr).$$
--
--   The radius $\tfrac{h}{2}\sqrt d$ is half the diagonal of the cube, so this inclusion is sharp. It is the standard passage from the maximum norm to the Euclidean norm and is invoked constantly when a cube of side $h$ must be shown to fit inside a cone, a ball, or a shrunken set.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The statement carries a hypothesis $0 \le h$ which is explicitly *unused* in the proof (it is bound to an underscored name), so the inclusion in fact holds for every real $h$; for $h < 0$ the cube is empty and the ball has negative radius, and both sides degenerate consistently.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/dbenbenn/quadratic-forms-sobolev/blob/7a1a680db2124d46ce370c91fd450aa454edf491/QuadraticFormsSobolev/Cubes.lean#L178-L185

